MAIL -- the mailbox directory=============================`mail' -- the UUCP mail reader and sender -- writes the mailbox as the bareLOGIN NAME.  `mail tester' takes a message from the keyboard, a line holdingone dot ending it, and files it as MAIL/tester; `mail' on its own opens whatis waiting there and prompts, one message at a time.  It also wants a scratchdevice at /r0: a real OS-9 system has a RAM disk there, and under os9exec`mount -r=256k /r0' makes one.  DOC/README-RUNNING lists the twenty programsthat want it.This directory ships with the collection, so `mail' does not have to makeit.ANOTHER CONVENTION, and it is the one that catches people out.  `rmail'and `mailx' -- the UUCP delivery program and the ELM mail reader -- wantthe mailbox to be a DIRECTORY named for the user, not a file.  Runeither and it says so in its own words:    rmail: dolocal: can't change to mailbox:           /dd/SPOOL/MAIL/tester/tester...error #214    mailx: can't change to your mailbox directory: 'su'...error 214Note what mailx asks for: it looks the user up by NUMBER, and thesuper-user 0.0's first entry in SYS/password is `su'.  Neither isbroken and neither is given what it wants; the folder they are pointedat is a plain file, which is what `mail', `messages' and `frm' expect.Measured 2026-09-21.